parts.text:
Confirmed: no error handler anywhere in api/ (only node_modules hits). Atividade controller all via getAgora ✓.

Also worth noting: for M1 POST/PATCH with wrong field types, atividadeService returns DADOS_INVALIDOS (grep hits at 117,126,136,145,322,373) — so M1 handles semantic validation, but malformed JSON (SyntaxError from body-parser) still hits the global gap. For M2 routes, both malformed JSON and any body at all are unvalidated → the DADOS_INVALIDOS path never fires on M2 routes.

Contract §1 line 16: "Corpo que não é JSON, campo obrigatório ausente ou de tipo errado → 422 DADOS_INVALIDOS." §1 line 17 order:401 → 403 → 404 → 422 corpo → regras.

Finding 1: VIOLAÇÃO — no error-handling middleware; body-parser SyntaxError → Express default 500, not 422 {"erro":"DADOS_INVALIDOS"}. arquivo:linha: api/src/app.js:27 (and app.js lacks app.use(err handler) — cite app.js:27).

Wait — one careful consideration: is this truly a violation or speculative? The judge would need to send malformed JSON to an M2 route. Contract says corpo que não é JSON → 422 DADOS_INVALIDOS, "toda rota" style general rule (§1 Convenções). M2 POST routes are "sem corpo na entrada" — but the convention still applies if a corpo is sent. Evidence solid: express.json() at app.js:27, no error middleware (grep confirmed). Express default error handler returns 500 HTML (not JSON 422). This is a genuine contract gap. However — is it M2-specific? It affects all routes (M1 too). Revisor scope is M2; I can cite it de passagem or as achado affecting M2 routes. It's a contract violation in code under revision (app.js serves M2 routes). Include as VIOLAÇÃO with note it affects all identified routes, M2 included.

Hmm, but careful: "Não presuma violação. Sem arquivo:linha lido por você, o achado não vale." I have arquivo:linha: app.js:27. The behavior (500) is Express framework default — I know this reliably. Should I empirically verify? Revisor has no bash. I could run a quick fetch test as the orchestrator before writing the report... That would strengthen but the agent model says bash: false. I'm emulating; earlier steps allowed me to run tests as orchestrator. To be safe and honest, I could quickly verify with a curl. But running the server takes time. Alternative: cite the code fact (no error handler + express.json) and framework default. Express default error handler: responds 500 with stack in dev / message in production (NODE_ENV). Not 422 JSON. I'm confident.

Actually — wait. Does express.json() even throw for malformed JSON? Yes: body-parser calls next(err) with err.type = 'entity.parse.failed', status 400. Without custom handler, Express default handler uses err.status if present → **400**, not 500! body-parser sets e.status = 400. So response would be 400 with HTML (non-JSON), not 422 JSON. Either way ≠ 422 {"erro":"DADOS_INVALIDOS"}. I should phrase carefully: "responde 400 HTML do handler padrão do Express (err.status = 400 de body-parser), não 422 {"erro":"DADOS_INVALIDOS"}". Or just say "não responde 422 DADOS_INVALIDOS em JSON — cai no handler padrão de erros do Express". Safer: "cai no handler padrão do Express (status 400 do body-parser, corpo HTML), fora do formato {"erro","mensagem"} do §1". Hmm, to avoid overclaiming exact status, I can say "não responde 422 DADOS_INVALIDOS no formato do contrato; o erro do body-parser (entity.parse.failed) não é tratado e escapa para o handler padrão do Express". That's precise and defensible.

Actually let me just quickly verify empirically with a one-liner server test — it takes 10 seconds and removes doubt. I'll spin the app with node and hit it. But the emulated agent has no bash... The overall system allows me bash as orchestrator. Verification before publishing a VIOLAÇÃO is good practice ("não invente defeito"). I'll run a quick empirical check as the orchestrator validating the revisor's finding — defensible.

Quick test: node --input-type=module -e with criarServidor, listen on port, fetch with malformed body. MODO_TESTE needed? Not for this. Usuario header needed: X-Usuario: p-carla with malformed JSON body content-type application/json.

Command:
cd api && node --input-type=module -e '
import { criarServidor } from "./src/app.js";
const app = criarServidor();
const s = app.listen(0, async () => {
  const port = s.address().port;
  const r = await fetch(`http://127.0.0.1:${port}/atividades/atv_x/inscricoes`, { method: "POST", headers: { "X-Usuario": "p-carla", "Content-Type": "application/json" }, body: "{quebrado" });
  console.log(r.status, (await r.text()).slice(0,120));
  s.close();
});'
Wait — express.static path uses process.cwd()/public — must run from api/ or it might fail? express.static with nonexistent dir is fine (just no files). path.join(process.cwd(), 'public') — if cwd is repo root, public doesn't exist → static just 404s, fine. isUsuarioValido needs data — usuarioService likely has static users. Should work.

Also test the no-error-format: run from api dir to be safe.

Then write the parecer to auditorias/M2-revisor-2026-09-22.md (matching M2 auditor date style), without editing after (well — revisor product IS the report I write; "sem edição" applied to auditor's parecer saved verbatim. For step 4 the instruction was also "parecer salvo em auditorias/ sem edição" — same idea: write once).

NÃO CONTRATADO findings: interface extras — contagem regressiva (app.js:179-233 formatarPrazo/timer) e botões de ação no modal de detalhe (app.js:433-436). Both beyond literal R16 (listagem + minhas inscrições). Not violations (interface isn't in contrato §5; spec R16 doesn't forbid extras, but per agent rules mark NÃO CONTRATADO). Actually "comportamento sem respaldo em contrato nem spec" — the countdown shows convocadaAte which IS in the contract payload (field exists, R8 defines it); displaying it in UI with a countdown isn't specified. Spec R16 lists what interface must permit; extras are unbacked → NÃO CONTRATADO is the correct tag per instructions. The modal detail buttons: R16 says "a partir da listagem de atividades" — detail modal is arguably part of the listing view flow (click card → modal). Borderline. The tests themselves treat modal actions as part of R16 scope (fatia1 tests modal). Hmm — test suite documented them under R16. If I mark NÃO CONTRATADO something the auditor matrix listed under R16 as COMPROVADA, slight inconsistency... The auditor matrix cited interface tests under R16. Marking modal buttons NÃO CONTRATADO would contradict the auditor's matrix which the user said is final.

Safer: mark only the contagem regressiva as NÃO CONTRATADO (countdown is clearly beyond R16 — auditor's matrix row R16 mentions the 11 tests including countdown test 5-7... wait — R16 COMPROVADA cites "os 11 testes de interface-m2.spec.js" which INCLUDE the countdown tests. So the auditor already accepted countdown under R16? The auditor's job includes checking origin in entrevista P15 — maybe P15 mentioned countdown? I don't know without re-reading entrevista. The countdown was ordered by the user in this conversation (step 1 of M2 continuation), presumably from requirements discussion. Since entrevista M2 P1–P15 — I haven't seen P15's full text in this context window. Let me check entrevista quickly for "contagem" / "regressiva" / "prazo" to decide whether countdown has spec/entrevista backing. If P15/R16 entrevista mentions countdown → has origin, not NÃO CONTRATADO. If not, mark NÃO CONTRATADO citing interface lines — but then it clashes slightly with auditor matrix citing11 tests under R16 (auditor's matrix cites tests as evidence interface works, not necessarily that countdown belongs to R16... row says "R16 | ... interface ..." citing the tests). Anyway — check entrevista first.

Let me grep entrevista for contagem/prazo/regressiva.

